            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you about the hospital course of your 18-year-old male patient, admitted on 12th February 2013 with acute right iliac fossa pain, nausea, and low-grade fever, clinically suspicious for appendicitis. Diagnostic workup including laboratory investigations revealed elevated inflammatory markers (CRP, leukocytosis), and abdominal ultrasound confirmed appendiceal inflammation. The clinical picture was consistent with acute appendicitis. The patient underwent laparoscopic appendectomy on 13th February 2013 without intraoperative complications. Histopathological examination confirmed acute appendicitis. Postoperative recovery was uneventful. The patient was mobilised early, tolerated oral intake well, and received perioperative antibiotic prophylaxis. Wound healing was satisfactory at discharge on 15th February 2013. I recommend wound inspection in your practice within one week. Please do not hesitate to contact me if further information is required.</div>"
            },
